  "question": {
    "text": "If the three planes $x=5,2 x-5 a y+3 z-2=0$ and $3 b x+y-3 z=0$ contain a common line, then $(a, b)$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "smYLb",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{8}{15},-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "mAluM",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{1}{5},-\\frac{8}{15}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "Xanko",
      "text": "\n$\\left(-\\frac{8}{15}, \\frac{1}{5}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cxBze",
      "text": "\n$\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}, \\frac{8}{15}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let the direction ratios of the common line be $\\ell, m$ and $n$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& \\therefore \\ell \\times 1+\\mathrm{m} \\times 0+\\mathrm{n} \\times 0=0 \\Rightarrow \\ell=0 \\\\\n& 2 \\ell-5 m a+3 n=0 \\Rightarrow 5 m a-3 n=0 \\\\\n& 3 \\ell b+m-3 n=0 \\Rightarrow m-3 n=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubtracting (3) from (1), we get $m(5 a-1)=0$\nNow, value of $m$ can not be zero because if $m=0$ then $n=0$\n$\\Rightarrow \\ell=m=n=0$ which is not possible.\nHence, $5 a-1=0 \\Rightarrow a=\\frac{1}{5}$",
    "image": null
  },
